---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Eyebrow from "../components/ui/Eyebrow.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy · opchain"
  description="What opchain.dev collects, how it's used, and how to opt out."
  active="/privacy"
>
  <main class="page">
    <div class="page-head">
      <Eyebrow>privacy</Eyebrow>
      <h1>Privacy.</h1>
    </div>

    <p class="lede">
      We collect the minimum needed to make the site useful. No ads, no
      cross-site tracking, no dark patterns.
    </p>
    <p class="updated">Last updated: September 2, 2026</p>

    <section>
      <h2>What we collect</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Cloudflare Web Analytics</strong> — cookieless pageview
          metrics such as URL, referrer, country, browser, and device class.
          It uses no advertising cookie or cross-site profile and runs on
          every page.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Email sign-ups.</strong> If you submit an install/download
          form or the newsletter form, Cloudflare KV stores your email plus
          any role, team-size, project description, source, timestamp, and
          request id you submitted or generated. The signup IP is not retained
          in the lead record. Truncated IP hashes support short-lived abuse
          controls. When operational analytics are configured, signup events
          use an email hash and download events use a separate full SHA-256
          IP-derived identifier; vendor retention applies. The lead record
          expires after 365 days. Email
          <a href="mailto:privacy@opchain.dev">privacy@opchain.dev</a> to
          delete yours sooner.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hosted MCP checkpoints.</strong> If an MCP client calls
          <code>write_checkpoint</code>, the client-supplied JSON is stored in
          Cloudflare KV for 30 days. Checkpoints are limited to 64 KiB and
          keyed by the skill plus a private signed session token returned by
          <code>create_checkpoint_session</code>; invented tokens cannot read
          or pre-seed state. Session creates and writes are rate-limited. Do
          not put secrets or regulated data in checkpoints. The v1.9 security
          migration deletes all pre-v1.9 hosted checkpoints; local on-disk
          checkpoints are unaffected.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Roadmap activity.</strong> Vote totals are aggregate and
          retained; a truncated hash of your IP is kept for 25 hours to stop
          duplicate daily votes. A feature request creates a public GitHub
          issue after submission, so do not include private information.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rate-limit counters.</strong> Short-lived counters derived
          from your IP prevent abuse of write endpoints. They expire between
          60 seconds and 25 hours, depending on the endpoint.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Browser analytics (optional).</strong> If you accept the
          consent banner, the PostHog browser SDK records pageviews and site
          interactions using browser local storage. Session recording is
          disabled. Declining means the browser SDK does not load.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Server-side usage events.</strong> When configured, the
          Worker sends PostHog events for downloads, feedback, and email
          sign-ups independently of the browser consent choice. Identifiers
          are SHA-256 hashes of the submitted email or IP-derived value; raw
          email is not sent to PostHog.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Feedback submissions</strong> go to Linear for triage.
          Including your email is optional; if you leave it blank we have no
          way to reply.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Visitor identifier.</strong> The <code>oc_id</code> cookie
          is a random UUID retained for one year so feature-flag rollouts stay
          consistent in the same browser. It is not used for advertising.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What we don't do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Sell your email or use it for advertising.</li>
        <li>Send your raw email to analytics vendors.</li>
        <li>Profile you across other sites.</li>
        <li>Record your screen or keystrokes.</li>
        <li>Sell usage data.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Opting out</h2>
      <p>
        Click <strong>Manage privacy</strong> in the footer to reset
        your browser-consent choice, PostHog local storage, and the
        <code>oc_id</code> cookie. Declining stops the browser PostHog SDK;
        it does not disable the limited server-side operational events
        described above. For deletion of an email record or a hosted MCP
        checkpoint before its TTL, email
        <a href="mailto:privacy@opchain.dev">privacy@opchain.dev</a> with the
        relevant email or checkpoint skill and session token.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Self-hosting</h2>
      <p>
        opchain is open source. In a fork, KV records and checkpoints stay in
        the operator's Cloudflare account; configured feedback, roadmap, and
        analytics flows go to services the operator selects. Analytics are
        env-gated: server events require
        <code>POSTHOG_PROJECT_API_KEY</code>, while consented browser events
        require both <code>PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY</code> and
        <code>PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST</code>. The deploy wrapper enables neither for
        self-hosted forks unless the operator explicitly supplies those
        settings. PostHog <code>*.i.posthog.com</code> hosts are already allowed
        by the Content Security Policy; a custom analytics domain must also be
        added to <code>script-src</code> and <code>connect-src</code> in the
        operator's policy.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem 1.5rem 6rem;
  }
  .page-head { margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }
  .page-head h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--fs-2xl);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-tight);
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
  }
  .lede { color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 1.5rem; max-width: 42rem; }
  .updated { color: var(--subtle); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--fs-xs); }
  section { margin-top: 1.75rem; }
  section h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--fs-lg);
    color: var(--text);
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-tight);
  }
  section p,
  section li { color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--fs-sm); line-height: var(--lh-base); }
  section ul { margin: 0.25rem 0 0 1.1rem; padding: 0; }
  section li { margin-bottom: 0.45rem; }
  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 0.5px solid var(--border);
    padding: 1px 5px;
    border-radius: var(--r-xs);
    color: var(--text);
  }
  section a { color: var(--accent); }
</style>
